To sort the divs inside the `id="things"` container based on the selected sorting option (title or updated date), you can use JavaScript to listen for changes in the select element and then sort the divs accordingly. Here's a simple implementation using vanilla JavaScript:

```html
<!-- Your existing HTML code here -->

<script>
  const sortSelect = document.getElementById('sort-select');
  const thingsContainer = document.getElementById('things');
  const things = Array.from(thingsContainer.children);

  sortSelect.addEventListener('change', () => {
    const sortBy = sortSelect.value;
    things.sort((a, b) => {
      if (sortBy === 'titleAZ') {
        return a.querySelector('.title').textContent.localeCompare(b.querySelector('.title').textContent);
      } else if (sortBy === 'titleZA') {
        return b.querySelector('.title').textContent.localeCompare(a.querySelector('.title').textContent);
      } else if (sortBy === 'newestFirst') {
        return new Date(b.querySelector('.updated-date').textContent) - new Date(a.querySelector('.updated-date').textContent);
      } else if (sortBy === 'oldestFirst') {
        return new Date(a.querySelector('.updated-date').textContent) - new Date(b.querySelector('.updated-date').textContent);
      }
    });

    thingsContainer.innerHTML = '';
    things.forEach(thing => thingsContainer.appendChild(thing));
  });
</script>
```

In this code, we first select the sort select element and the container that holds the divs. We then convert the childNodes of the container into an array for easier manipulation.

We add an event listener to the sort select element to listen for changes. When a change occurs, we determine the selected sorting option and sort the array of divs accordingly. We use the `localeCompare` method for string comparison and the `new Date` constructor for date comparison.

Finally, we clear the container and append the sorted divs back into it.

