When you copy content from one place and paste it into an editable div, the styling of the source content is often carried over due to the way the clipboard data is handled by the browser. This can be problematic if you want the pasted content to conform to the styling of your webpage.

To avoid this, you can use a technique called "paste normalization" or "paste sanitization". This involves intercepting the paste event, removing any unwanted styling from the clipboard data, and then pasting the plain text into the editable div.

Here's an example of how you can implement paste normalization using JavaScript:

```html
<div contentEditable="true" id="editableDiv"></div>

<script>
  const editableDiv = document.getElementById('editableDiv');

  editableDiv.addEventListener('paste', (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();

    // Get the clipboard data
    const clipboardData = event.clipboardData || window.clipboardData;

    // Get the text from the clipboard data
    const pastedData = clipboardData.getData('text/plain');

    // Insert the plain text into the editable div
    editableDiv.innerHTML = pastedData;
  });
